  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Sunday: The cold front will continue to exit the \nregion through the early morning. Colder will continue to move \ninto the region and bring snow levels down to 4000-5000 ft by \nmid morning. Short term models are keeping shower chances \nthrough today for the higher terrain with a chance of mountain\nsnow. Precip amounts are not expected to be very much as \nprobability for a tenth of an inch range from 10-30 percent. \nSnow amounts are generally up 1-2 inches but pockets of up to 4 \ninches are possible. Hikers and campers in the higher elevations\nshould plan accordingly. By Sunday, the Low stalls over \nSouthwest Conus and high pressure starts to nudge into the \nregion from the West. Precip tapers off by Sunday afternoon. \nSaturday and Sunday highs will be in the upper 40s and low 60s. \nOvernight lows will be in the 30s to low 40s. Many areas will be\nin the upper 20s to low 30s Sunday night into Monday morning. \nIt will be a good to time to start putting away the hoses and \nsprinklers. \n\nWinds will continue to weaken behind the front and switch \nnortherly by this morning bringing gusty north winds of 25-35 \nmph through the Okanogan Valley and Waterville Plateau. Breezy \nconditions continue Sunday with gusty northeast winds across \nmuch of the region. Speeds will be slightly lower than Friday \nwith gusts 25-35 mph across the Basin and Idaho Panhandle.\n\nMonday through Friday: Ensembles are becoming better aligned \nfor the upcoming week. The Low is expected to stay over \nSouthwest Conus while ridging builds along the coast line \nthrough Wednesday. Ensembles are hinting at the ridge moving \neast as another trough begins to press into the region. It is \nnot looking to bring any precip by the end of the week. The \nInland Northwest will be under a dry fall like pattern with near\nnormal temperatures. Highs will be in the upper 40s to low 60s \nfor most. Some areas in the lower Central Basin could approach \n70. Overnight lows will continue to chilly with a range of 30s \nto low 40s. /JDC",
